### 01-00-introduction-context.md
[1] sentence: Our project asks how a university can see this drift early enough to offer help within the same semester, using only records it already holds.
[2] sentence: Section 1.1 explains why the problem matters and who would use the results, and Section 1.2 fixes the scope and the project objective.

### 01-01-motivation-stakeholders.md
[3] sentence: The late moment of notice described in Section 1 matters because support works only while a student can still catch up, and because the people who could give that support each see a different part of the picture.
[4] sentence: The problem is therefore worth analysing for two reasons: the records that could show the drift early already exist, and several groups of staff would act on them if they had them in a usable form.

### 01-02-scope-objective.md
[5] sentence: The stakeholder needs of Section 1.1, a short and timely list for staff and a view of where students are lost for the program, set the boundaries of the project: what lies inside and outside them, the objective we pursue, the data that stand in for the university's records, and how far the project has gone.
[6] sentence: Our objective is to turn records the university already holds into two things staff do not have today: a short, timely list of students to contact at each review point, with an observed reason for each name, and patterns by course and student group that show the program where and when students are lost.
[7] sentence: The objective serves the Business Goal of Section 2, to improve successful course completion by identifying students who stop taking part early enough to support them within the semester.
[8] label: **How far the project goes.**
[9] sentence: The four analytical questions of Section 3 run from description to a ranking for action: AQ1 describes how enrolments end, AQ2 examines which early signs go with non-completion, AQ3 tests how well non-completion can be predicted early, and AQ4 ranks students on a list of fixed length.
[10] sentence: Milestone 4 completed the mandatory Level 1 for all four questions, that is, the modeling strategy, candidate methods, rationale and evaluation design, and specified a Level 2 experiment as a protocol without running it, a choice of scope explained in Section 10.
[11] sentence: On this basis we analysed AQ1 and AQ2 on the 9 module runs of 2013, the development years of the design, following the analysis plan that Milestone 4 fixed before any result was seen; the 13 runs of 2014 stay unopened, because they form the single test set of AQ3, and the only 2014 figures used are the outcome counts per run that Milestone 3 had already published.
[12] sentence: AQ3 and AQ4 are therefore reported as proposals with their expected outputs, in the proposal tense, and no model metric is reported for them (Sections 8–11).
[13] label: **The project as one chain.**
