[86] sentence: For AQ1 and AQ2 the design has now been applied to the 2013 runs, and Section 11 reports whether their criteria were met; for AQ3 and AQ4 it is the evaluation that the proposed model and ranking would undergo, and no metric value exists yet.
[87] sentence: Table 9.1 summarises the Evaluation Plan for the four questions: the primary metric, the baseline it is compared with, the validation, and the success criterion fixed in advance.
[88] sentence: Section 9.1 gives the full validation design and criteria, and Section 9.2 the leakage, fairness and interpretability checks.

### 09-01-validation-success.md
[89] sentence: The Evaluation Plan of Section 9 names one primary metric and one success criterion per question; the validation behind each and the reasons for the thresholds follow, question by question.

### 09-02-fairness-interpretability.md
[90] sentence: The validation design of Section 9.1 decides whether the proposed model beats the rule; three further checks decide whether its result can be trusted and used: that no test information leaked into it, that its errors do not fall unfairly on a group of students, and that staff can see why a student is listed.

### 10-00-experimental-status.md
[91] sentence: The evaluation design of Section 9 is complete for all four questions, and its implementation status differs between them.
[92] sentence: This project completed Level 1 of the modeling stage, the compulsory level: the modeling strategy of Section 8 and the evaluation design of Section 9.
[93] sentence: The optional Level 2 prototype, a predictive experiment for AQ3 and AQ4, was specified in full in Milestone 4 as a protocol and has not been run (Milestone 4, Section 7).
[94] sentence: No model has been trained, so no model performance, metric, chart or confusion matrix exists for AQ3 or AQ4; their expected outputs are described in Section 11.4.
[95] sentence: Leaving the prototype unrun is our choice of scope, for two reasons.
[96] sentence: The brief makes Level 2 optional, so Level 1 completes the modeling stage.
[97] sentence: In addition, the 13 runs of 2014 are the project's only independent test and can be used once: we keep them for one complete run of the frozen protocol, from the baselines to the logged test, so that no 2014 result is seen before the model is frozen, and a partial early run would spend that test.
[98] label: **What was computed instead.**
[99] sentence: In Milestone 5 we ran the AQ1 and AQ2 analyses on the 9 runs of 2013, following the methods and criteria that Milestone 4 fixed before any result.
[100] sentence: These are descriptive and diagnostic analyses, grouped rates with intervals and an association model, not the Level 2 modeling prototype: they train no predictive model, open no 2014 outcome and make no claim about how well non-completion can be predicted.
[101] sentence: Their results are reported with the findings, in Section 11.2 for AQ1 and Section 11.3 for AQ2, and their methods are those of Section 8.1.
[102] sentence: On the brief's scale the project therefore stands at Level 1: the AQ1 and AQ2 analyses are the analysis results of this report, not a Level 2 experiment.

### 11-00-findings-overview.md
[103] sentence: Section 10 states that no predictive prototype has been run, so the evaluation design of Section 9 stays a proposal for the two predictive and prescriptive questions.
[104] sentence: In contrast, the descriptive question (AQ1) and the diagnostic question (AQ2) needed no model training and no access to the 2014 test runs, so we ran them on the 9 module runs of 2013 exactly as Milestone 4 had fixed them before any result existed (Sections 8.1 and 9.1).
[105] sentence: The findings return to the four final Analytical Questions of Section 3 and answer each one at the level of evidence the project actually reached: AQ1 and AQ2 with real results on their stated populations, AQ3 and AQ4 with the outputs they are expected to produce and the success criteria that would decide them.
[106] sentence: Building on these answers, Section 11.5 relates them to the decisions the results can support for the program manager, the lecturer-advisors and the support office (D1–D3, Section 2.1).
[107] sentence: The confidence given to each answer uses three grades.
[108] sentence: High means that the counts are verified by two independent computations on the stated population, so the answer is computationally verified for that population; it says nothing about validity for the university's courses.
[109] sentence: Moderate means that the estimates keep their direction under every check run, while they rest on one year, on a model whose fit has a known weakness or on associations that unrecorded reasons could explain.
[110] sentence: Not applicable means that no result exists yet.
[111] sentence: The Final Project Traceability Matrix in Section 11.1 places these four answers on the full chain from goal to decision.
[112] sentence: Sections 11.2 and 11.3 then report the AQ1 and AQ2 results, Section 11.4 the expected outputs of AQ3 and AQ4, and Section 11.5 the answers side by side with the decisions they support.

